    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Berkshire (BRK.B) is basically flat for 2026. It's up about 0.6% for the year, while the S&P 500 is up about 14%.\n\n| | Price | vs. year start |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dec 31, 2025 close | $502.65 | \u2014 |\n| Today (Sep 26, midday) | $505.48 | **+0.6%** |\n| Lowest close (Apr 23) | $465.40 | \u22127.4% |\n| Highest close (Aug 17) | $529.42 | +5.3% |\n\n**How the year went:**\n- **January:** It slid from about $500 to about $474 by late January.\n- **February and March:** It bounced back above $500 in early February, then lost ground again. It closed around $468 on March 30.\n- **April:** This was the low point, about 7% down for the year.\n- **May to mid-August:** It climbed steadily and hit a closing high of $529 on August 17 (the intraday high was $537.74 that day).\n- **Since then:** It dropped back into the $500\u2013510 range, about 4.5% below the August high.\n\n**Compared with the market:** SPY, an S&P 500 fund, has gone from about $677 to $771 this year, a gain of roughly 14%. So Berkshire has lagged the index by about 13 percentage points. The SPY start price looks adjusted for dividends and Berkshire pays none, so the gap is approximate. Either way, Berkshire has trailed by a lot. Its price has also stayed in a fairly narrow band, roughly $465 to $530, all year.\n\nThese numbers are for the Class B shares. Class A shares (BRK.A) track them very closely in percentage terms."
